  </section>

  <section id="projects" class="projects">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="section-tag">
        Projects
      </p>

      <div class="project-showcase">
        <div class="project-copy">
          <p class="project-kicker">
            Featured Build
          </p>

          <h2>
            LogPulse / Weekly Work Summarizer
          </h2>

          <p>
            A privacy-first Chrome extension that generates weekly engineering
            status reports from GitHub, Jira, and Google Calendar activity using
            a local LLM.
          </p>

          <div class="project-metrics">
            <span>~5 sec generation</span>
            <span>$0 API cost</span>
            <span>Zero remote data transmission</span>
          </div>

          <div class="project-actions">
            <a href="#case-studies" class="primary">
              Read Case Study
            </a>
            <a href="#prd" class="secondary">
              View PRD
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="project-demo">
          <video controls preload="metadata" playsinline>
            <source src={logPulseDemo} type="video/mp4" />
            Your browser does not support the video tag.
          </video>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="project-showcase">
        <div class="project-copy">
          <p class="project-kicker">
            Featured Build
          </p>

          <h2>
            IntelliQuery / Document Intelligence Assistant
          </h2>

          <p>
            An AI-powered RAG experience that turns uploaded PDFs into an
            interactive knowledge assistant with grounded answers, citations,
            and fast semantic search.
          </p>

          <div class="project-metrics">
            <span>&lt;5 sec indexing</span>
            <span>3–5 sec responses</span>
            <span>100% grounded answers</span>
          </div>

          <div class="project-actions">
            <a href="#case-studies-2" class="primary">
              Read Case Study
            </a>
            <a href="#prd-2" class="secondary">
              View PRD
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="project-demo">
          <video controls preload="metadata" playsinline>
            <source src={videoProjectA} type="video/mp4" />
            Your browser does not support the video tag.
          </video>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="case-studies" class="case-study">
    <div class="container case-study-layout">
      <aside class="case-study-aside">
        <p class="section-tag">
          Case Study
        </p>

        <h2>
          Automating the weekly status report with a zero-data-leakage Chrome extension.
        </h2>

        <p>
          How I designed and built Weekly Work Summarizer, a privacy-first AI tool
          that turns GitHub, Jira, and Google Calendar activity into a concise weekly
          update using a local LLM.
        </p>

        <div class="impact-strip">
          <div>
            <strong>~5 sec</strong>
            <span>summary generation</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <strong>$0</strong>
            <span>cloud LLM fees</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <strong>100%</strong>
            <span>local data processing</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </aside>

      <article class="case-study-story">
        <section>
          <span>01</span>
          <h3>Executive Summary</h3>
          <p>
            Engineers spend hours every Friday gathering scattered commit logs,
            Jira tickets, and calendar events just to write a weekly status update.
            The process is tedious, easy to under-report, and risky when internal
            work is pasted into cloud-based LLMs.
          </p>
          <p>
            Weekly Work Summarizer solves this by aggregating activity across
            GitHub, Jira, and Google Calendar, truncating and sanitizing the data
            client-side, and routing it through a local LLM. The result is a precise
            4-6 bullet weekly summary in under 5 seconds, with no external API fees
            or server overhead.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <span>02</span>
          <h3>The Problem</h3>
          <p>
            Writing weekly status reports, standup blurbs, and 1:1 summaries is a
            recurring high-friction workflow for software engineers and technical leads.
            Work is fragmented across Git commit history, Jira task updates, and
            Google Calendar meetings, forcing developers to context-switch across
            dashboards for 30-45 minutes every Friday.
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>Manual log scraping causes developers to miss bug fixes, PR reviews, or technical discussions.</li>
            <li>Cloud LLM copy-pasting creates privacy and NDA risks for proprietary work.</li>
            <li>Raw API exports create payload bloat, token overflow, slow generation, and hallucination risk.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section>
          <span>03</span>
          <h3>The Goal</h3>
          <p>
            Build a lightweight Chrome extension that fetches activity across GitHub,
            Jira, and Google Calendar, sanitizes and compresses the payload on the
            client, and generates a structured 4-6 bullet weekly summary via a local
            LLM in under 5 seconds, with zero data leaving the developer's machine.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <span>04</span>
          <h3>The AI Solution</h3>
          <p>
            The solution uses a zero-data-leakage pipeline powered by a lightweight
            local LLM. Instead of relying on cloud infrastructure or expensive
            fine-tuning, the architecture centers on client-side context engineering.
          </p>

          <div class="flow-line">
            <span>Fetch</span>
            <span>Sanitize</span>
            <span>Compress</span>
            <span>Summarize locally</span>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section>
          <span>05</span>
          <h3>Data & Context Strategy</h3>
          <p>
            The model avoids offline training and custom fine-tuning. It uses dynamic
            zero-shot context retrieval and clean, structured in-context learning.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <div>
              <dt>GitHub</dt>
              <dd>First-line commit headers from PushEvent and PR titles from PullRequestEvent over the last 7 days.</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Jira</dt>
              <dd>Key, Summary, and Status fields for issues updated in the last 7 days.</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Calendar</dt>
              <dd>Event summaries and titles, filtered to exclude declined invitations.</dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <section>
          <span>06</span>
          <h3>Time, Cost & Privacy Impact</h3>
          <p>
            The extension cuts status prep from 20-30 minutes to a single click,
            routes inference through a local LLM for $0 API cost, and eliminates
            data-leakage risk by keeping proprietary work fully on-device.
          </p>
        </section>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="case-studies-2" class="case-study">
    <div class="container case-study-layout">
      <aside class="case-study-aside">
        <p class="section-tag">
          Case Study
        </p>

        <h2>
          IntelliQuery: An AI-powered document intelligence assistant for accurate knowledge retrieval.
        </h2>

        <p>
          How I built a retrieval-augmented generation app that turns static PDFs into an interactive
          knowledge assistant with grounded answers, source citations, and fast semantic search.
        </p>

        <div class="impact-strip">
          <div>
            <strong>&lt;5 sec</strong>
            <span>PDF indexing</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <strong>3–5 sec</strong>
            <span>response generation</span>
          </div>
          <div>
            <strong>100%</strong>
            <span>document-grounded answers</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </aside>

      <article class="case-study-story">
        <section>
          <span>01</span>
          <h3>Executive Summary</h3>
          <p>
            IntelliQuery transforms static PDF documents into a conversational knowledge assistant.
            Users can upload one or more documents, ask natural-language questions, and receive
            accurate answers grounded in the uploaded content with source citations for verification.
          </p>
          <p>
            The product combines semantic retrieval, local embeddings, and a large language model
            to make information discovery faster and more intuitive while reducing the need for
            manual document scanning.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <span>02</span>
          <h3>The Problem</h3>
          <p>
            Teams and individuals often rely on long PDFs such as policies, handbooks, technical docs,
            and research papers to find critical information. Traditional keyword search is slow,
            brittle, and often misses contextually relevant answers.
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li>Manual browsing through long documents wastes time and creates repetitive effort.</li>
            <li>Keyword search often returns irrelevant results or misses answers expressed differently.</li>
            <li>Users need trustworthy responses that can be verified against the original document.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section>
          <span>03</span>
          <h3>The Goal</h3>
          <p>
            Build an intelligent document assistant that allows users to ask questions naturally and
            receive context-aware answers grounded solely in the uploaded documents, with transparent
            citations and a smooth experience for multi-document workflows.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section>
          <span>04</span>
          <h3>The AI Solution</h3>
          <p>
            IntelliQuery uses a retrieval-augmented generation pipeline. Uploaded PDFs are extracted,
            chunked, embedded, and indexed in ChromaDB, then queried semantically to find the most
            relevant passages before generating a response with Llama 3.2 through Ollama.
          </p>

          <div class="flow-line">
            <span>Upload PDFs</span>
            <span>Extract text</span>
            <span>Embed & index</span>
            <span>Retrieve & answer</span>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section>
          <span>05</span>
          <h3>Architecture & Stack</h3>
          <p>
            The application was designed as a modular, production-ready system that separates ingestion,
            retrieval, and generation layers while keeping the experience lightweight and intuitive.
          </p>

          <dl>
            <div>
              <dt>Frontend</dt>
              <dd>React, TypeScript, Vite, and Tailwind CSS for a modern chat-style experience.</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>Backend</dt>
              <dd>Python and FastAPI for document processing, indexing, and query orchestration.</dd>
            </div>
            <div>
              <dt>AI Layer</dt>
              <dd>Llama 3.2 via Ollama, Nomic Embed Text, ChromaDB, PyMuPDF, and vector similarity search.</dd>
            </div>
          </dl>
        </section>

        <section>
          <span>06</span>
          <h3>Results & Impact</h3>
          <p>
            The project demonstrated how RAG can make knowledge retrieval more accessible, trustworthy,
            and efficient. It reduced manual search time from minutes to seconds, improved answer
            grounding through retrieval-based context, and laid the foundation for future expansion
            into OCR, hybrid search, reranking, and conversation memory.
          </p>
        </section>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="prd" class="prd">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="section-tag">
        PRD
      </p>

      <h2>
        LogPulse - AI-powered weekly work summary generator.
      </h2>

      <p class="prd-intro">
        A completed product requirements document for a privacy-first Chrome extension
        that turns GitHub, Jira, and calendar activity into a concise weekly progress report.
      </p>

      <div class="prd-meta">
        <span>Author: Gayatri Gupta</span>
        <span>Status: Completed</span>
        <span>Date: July 24, 2026</span>
        <span>Target Release: NA</span>
      </div>

      <div class="prd-grid">
        <div>
          <h3>User Problem</h3>
          <p>
            Employees are expected to share weekly progress updates, but remembering
            every contribution across GitHub pull requests, commits, Jira stories,
            meetings, and day-to-day work is difficult. Important work can be missed,
            making it harder to accurately showcase impact.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>AI Value Proposition</h3>
          <p>
            LogPulse uses a foundation model to transform raw activity data into a
            concise, human-readable weekly summary. The LLM identifies significant
            contributions, groups related work, and generates structured bullet points
            that employees can quickly review and share.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>Persona & Workflow</h3>
          <p>
            Built for software developers and engineering leads. The Chrome extension
            collects weekly activity from connected tools, summarizes the most relevant
            work locally, and presents an editable status update for the user.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>Business Success Metric</h3>
          <p>
            Reduce weekly status update preparation from 20-30 minutes of manual
            compilation to approximately 15 seconds of automated summarization.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>Technical Architecture</h3>
          <p>
            The model strategy is prompt engineering with a local LLM. Data sources
            include GitHub, Jira, and employee calendar activity. All API fetching,
            parsing, context truncation, and inference occur on the local client machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>Privacy Strategy</h3>
          <p>
            LogPulse is designed for zero remote data transmission. Raw employee
            activity never leaves the client machine, preserving privacy while enabling
            offline-friendly summarization.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>Model Evaluation</h3>
          <p>
            Minimum viable performance requires at least 95% fact-recall precision,
            less than 1% hallucinated facts, strict zero fabrication, end-to-end latency
            under 5 seconds, and low or no operating cost.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>Fallback UX</h3>
          <p>
            Timeout state: "Request timed out." Low confidence state: "Model returned
            an unexpected output format. Displaying formatted raw activity log below."
            API failure state: "Failed to fetch."
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="prd-2" class="prd">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="section-tag">
        PRD
      </p>

      <h2>
        IntelliQuery - AI-powered document intelligence assistant.
      </h2>

      <p class="prd-intro">
        A completed product requirements document for a retrieval-augmented generation app that turns
        uploaded PDFs into an interactive, citation-backed knowledge assistant.
      </p>

      <div class="prd-meta">
        <span>Author: Gayatri Gupta</span>
        <span>Status: Completed</span>
        <span>Date: July 29, 2026</span>
        <span>Target Release: NA</span>
      </div>

      <div class="prd-grid">
        <div>
          <h3>Product Overview</h3>
          <p>
            IntelliQuery helps students, researchers, employees, and technical teams find information
            in long PDFs through natural-language questions instead of manual scanning or brittle keyword search.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>AI Value Proposition</h3>
          <p>
            The product uses a RAG architecture with Llama 3.2 to retrieve semantically relevant passages,
            generate grounded answers, and provide citations and page references for transparency.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>User Workflow</h3>
          <p>
            Users upload one or more PDFs, the system extracts and chunks the text, stores embeddings in ChromaDB,
            and then answers questions with document-grounded responses.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>Technical Architecture</h3>
          <p>
            The system combines PDF processing, semantic chunking, embedding generation, vector search,
            and Llama 3.2 inference through Ollama to deliver a modular and scalable experience.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>Performance Targets</h3>
          <p>
            The target experience includes under 5 seconds for typical PDF processing, under 2 seconds per document
            for embedding generation, under 500 ms for retrieval, and 3–5 seconds for end-to-end answers.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>Guardrails & Safety</h3>
          <p>
            The product is designed to reject unsupported files, avoid fabricated answers, handle unsupported queries,
            and provide graceful fallback messages when relevant information cannot be found.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  </main>
</Layout>


<style>
.about {
  padding:120px 0;
}


